## Summary: Fetching Relational Data

This guide covers how to fetch a primary data type (a **Category**) and all its related data (**Reviews**) from Strapi using a single, nested GraphQL query in a React application.

## Building the Nested GraphQL Query

To fetch a category and its associated reviews, you can structure your query to ask for related data within the primary data type.

-   **Query Variables:** The query accepts an `$id` of type `ID!` to specify which category to fetch.
-   **Primary Data:** It requests a single `category` matching the provided `id`.
-   **Nested Relational Data:** Within the `category` fields, you can request the `reviews` field, which Strapi automatically adds for relations. For each review, you can specify which fields you need (e.g., `title`, `rating`, `id`).
-   **Deeply Nested Data:** You can even go one level deeper and request the `categories` associated with each `review`. This is useful because a review might belong to multiple categories.

```graphql
query GetCategory($id: ID!) {
  category(id: $id) {
    name
    id
    reviews {
      title
      body
      rating
      id
      categories {
        name
        id
      }
    }
  }
}
```

> [!TIP]
> This nested query approach is highly efficient. It avoids the "N+1" problem by fetching all the data you need from the server in a single network request, rather than making a separate request for each related item.

## Implementing in React

1.  **Get URL Parameter:** Use the `useParams` hook from `react-router-dom` to extract the category `id` from the URL.
    ```jsx
    import { useParams } from 'react-router-dom';
    const { id } = useParams();
    ```

2.  **Execute Query:** Use the `useQuery` hook from Apollo Client, passing the GraphQL query and the `id` as a variable.
    ```jsx
    import { useQuery } from '@apollo/client';
    const { loading, error, data } = useQuery(GET_CATEGORY, {
      variables: { id: id }
    });
    ```

## Rendering the Nested Data

Once the data is fetched, you can render it in your component. The data structure will mirror your query structure.

-   **Category Title:** `data.category.name`
-   **Mapping Reviews:** Loop over the nested reviews array: `data.category.reviews.map(review => ...)`
-   **Mapping Review Categories:** Inside the review loop, map over each review's categories: `review.categories.map(c => ...)`

> [!IMPORTANT]
> When accessing data for a single item (like on a details page) versus a list, the data path changes. For a single review, you access its categories via `data.review.categories`, not by mapping, as there's only one top-level review object.
